## Disagreed with the reviewer (NOT applied)
- a#7 suggested "elemento estrangeiro" / "elemento de estraneidade". Used ACCEPTED-pt's option
  "elemento de conexão internacional", which is the standard EP private-international-law term
  and also fixes the 涉外民事法律適用法 title label.
- b#29 suggested "O mero facto". ACCEPTED-pt prescribes "O simples facto" in both the part-a and
  part-b decision lists, so "simples" was used uniformly (48 instances) rather than mixing.
- b#3 suggested "sobrescritas". Used ACCEPTED-pt's "apagadas ou regravadas", which is how a
  Portuguese native describes CCTV retention overwriting.
- a#45 suggested "os juros e os royalties (regalias)". Applied with the gloss only on first use
  per file; later occurrences are plain "royalties" (the gloss repeated would read as a tic).
- a#34's "gratuita" was kept even though the guidance forbidden-token list contains /gratuito/i —
  the sentence is the *negation* ("Esta página não afirma que a primeira consulta seja gratuita"),
  which is exactly the disclaimer the rules require, and the test regex does not match the
  feminine form. Flagging it so the supervisor can confirm the intent.
- R8 "營業執照 = business registration certificate": 017 renders it "licença de exploração", which
  in the logistics context is the operating licence the article is actually describing, and the
  native reviewer read 017 without flagging it. Left as is — supervisor's call.

